  Gap 2 (what you missed): How boundary resharding is done

  This is actually a significant efficiency difference.

  Phase 2's approach — all-gather then split:
  Stage 0 (tp=2):
    GPU0 holds activation[..., 0:H/2]   ← half hidden
    GPU1 holds activation[..., H/2:H]   ← half hidden

  BoundaryAllGather (before send):
    GPU0 all-gathers → holds full activation[..., 0:H]
    GPU1 all-gathers → holds full activation[..., 0:H]
    Both GPUs now hold FULL tensor (2× memory, wasted work)

  P2P send: GPU0 sends full tensor to GPU2, GPU1 sends full tensor to GPU3

  BoundarySplit (after recv, receiver tp=4):
    GPU2 keeps only chunk[0] = [..., 0:H/4]
    GPU3 keeps only chunk[1] = [..., H/4:H/2]
    GPU4 keeps only chunk[2] = [..., H/2:3H/4]
    GPU5 keeps only chunk[3] = [..., 3H/4:H]
  The cost: one full all-gather before every pipeline boundary even when only a reshard was needed.

  Alpa's approach — tile-to-tile multicast:
  Stage 0 (tp=2):
    GPU0 holds activation[..., 0:H/2]
    GPU1 holds activation[..., H/2:H]

  Tile correspondence math (no actual communication yet):
    dst GPU2 needs [..., 0:H/4]   ← comes from GPU0 (first half of GPU0's shard)
    dst GPU3 needs [..., H/4:H/2] ← comes from GPU0 (second half of GPU0's shard)
    dst GPU4 needs [..., H/2:3H/4]← comes from GPU1 (first half of GPU1's shard)
    dst GPU5 needs [..., 3H/4:H]  ← comes from GPU1 (second half of GPU1's shard)

  Direct P2P sends (no all-gather):
    GPU0 → GPU2: sends slice [..., 0:H/4]
    GPU0 → GPU3: sends slice [..., H/4:H/2]
    GPU1 → GPU4: sends slice [..., H/2:3H/4]
    GPU1 → GPU5: sends slice [..., 3H/4:H]
  The cost: only the actual bytes needed travel over the network. No intermediate all-gather.